  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a proposed reaction network for electrochemical CO2 reduction on a catalyst surface. Blue spheres represent surface sites holding adsorbed intermediates, while colored boxes group the final products by carbon number: C1 products in yellow, C2+ products in green, and H2 in red.\n\nAt the center, adsorbed CO2 is transformed through sequential proton/electron transfer steps into surface-bound oxygenated and carbon-containing intermediates. From these shared intermediates, the pathway branches toward several one-carbon products, including formic acid/formate, methanol/formaldehyde, methane, and CO. A competing hydrogen-evolution route is also shown, producing H2 from adsorbed hydrogen species.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes carbon–carbon coupling. Surface intermediates combine to form a two-carbon species leading to glyoxal, ethylene, and ethanol/acetaldehyde. Further reduction and coupling steps appear to extend this chemistry to three-carbon products, shown as 1-propanol and propionaldehyde. Multiple arrows connect these species, indicating that several products arise from common intermediates and can interconvert through additional hydrogenation or reduction.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a branching mechanistic map in which CO2 reduction proceeds through adsorbed intermediates toward a wide spectrum of C1, C2, and C3 products, with hydrogen evolution as a parallel side reaction.",
